1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,367
Picture Dana at her laptop on a slow Tuesday, deciding between two quotes.

2
00:00:04,367 --> 00:00:08,492
A production company wants three thousand dollars for a half-day shoot: four

3
00:00:08,492 --> 00:00:10,120
finished ads at the end of it.

4
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:14,679
An AI tool just generated twenty statics from the same brief, in an afternoon, for a

5
00:00:14,679 --> 00:00:15,873
fraction of that cost.

6
00:00:15,873 --> 00:00:20,216
Most people would look at that gap and conclude "AI made advertising free." That

7
00:00:20,216 --> 00:00:24,612
belief sounds true on the surface, and it's wrong in a way that costs real money.

8
00:00:24,612 --> 00:00:27,543
It quietly implies testing and judgement got free too.

9
00:00:27,543 --> 00:00:28,195
They didn't.

10
00:00:28,195 --> 00:00:32,048
Only one part of the job did, the part where the ad actually gets made.

11
00:00:32,048 --> 00:00:36,608
I want to spend this whole lecture on that gap, because almost every bad decision in

12
00:00:36,608 --> 00:00:40,027
this space traces back to collapsing those two claims into one.

13
00:00:40,027 --> 00:00:44,315
Someone hears "AI made ads free", assumes their testing budget is free too, and

14
00:00:44,315 --> 00:00:46,595
finds out the expensive way that it isn't.

15
00:00:47,145 --> 00:00:48,717
Look at what actually moved.

16
00:00:48,717 --> 00:00:53,434
Generating one more variant of an ad used to mean another day of a shoot, or another

17
00:00:53,434 --> 00:00:55,456
few hundred dollars to a freelancer.

18
00:00:55,456 --> 00:00:57,421
Now it's minutes and a few dollars.

19
00:00:57,421 --> 00:01:00,341
That's real, and it's the reason this course exists.

20
00:01:00,341 --> 00:01:02,082
But read the left column again.

21
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:05,732
Media spend to run an honest test still costs what it always did.

22
00:01:05,732 --> 00:01:09,831
A platform doesn't discount your budget because the creative was AI-made.

23
00:01:09,831 --> 00:01:14,548
Deciding which twenty angles are worth trying still takes judgement software doesn't

24
00:01:14,548 --> 00:01:19,209
have, because a generator will happily produce twenty confident variations of a bad

25
00:01:19,209 --> 00:01:21,343
idea just as fast as twenty good ones.

26
00:01:21,343 --> 00:01:26,004
And catching a claim that could get an ad account suspended still needs a human who

27
00:01:26,004 --> 00:01:30,384
knows the rules, arguably more urgently now, because it's never been easier to

28
00:01:30,384 --> 00:01:31,844
accidentally generate one.

29
00:01:31,844 --> 00:01:33,248
None of that got cheaper.

30
00:01:33,248 --> 00:01:37,740
If anything, it got more important, because there's more of it to check, in less

31
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:39,762
time, before it reaches an audience.

32
00:01:40,312 --> 00:01:43,220
Here's what that looks like against a real account.

33
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:47,554
Remember Harlan's numbers from the last section: fifty-eight dollars a pack,

34
00:01:47,554 --> 00:01:50,406
thirty-four dollars of margin before any ad spend.

35
00:01:50,406 --> 00:01:55,139
A proper video shoot easily runs into the thousands, split across whatever variants

36
00:01:55,139 --> 00:01:55,823
it produces.

37
00:01:55,823 --> 00:02:00,499
Amortise that over four ad variants and the cost per variant alone can eat most of

38
00:02:00,499 --> 00:02:04,947
one order's entire margin, before the ad has proven it can sell a single pack.

39
00:02:04,947 --> 00:02:09,623
Dana would have to sell dozens of packs just to earn back the shoot, regardless of

40
00:02:09,623 --> 00:02:11,106
whether the ad ever works.

41
00:02:11,106 --> 00:02:15,497
Twenty AI statics from one brief costs a small fraction of that shoot, total.

42
00:02:15,497 --> 00:02:16,409
Not per variant.

43
00:02:16,409 --> 00:02:16,752
Total.

44
00:02:16,752 --> 00:02:18,690
That's not a marginal improvement.

45
00:02:18,690 --> 00:02:23,424
It's the difference between an experiment Harlan can actually afford to run and one

46
00:02:23,424 --> 00:02:26,959
they can't, and it changes the entire posture of the decision.

47
00:02:26,959 --> 00:02:29,754
A shoot forces you to bet big on your best guess.

48
00:02:29,754 --> 00:02:34,487
A batch lets you stop betting on a guess at all, and test several honestly instead.

49
00:02:35,037 --> 00:02:39,563
I want to be straight about the part that doesn't get said in the "AI made ads free"

50
00:02:39,563 --> 00:02:39,994
version.

51
00:02:39,994 --> 00:02:44,519
When a variant costs almost nothing to make, it's tempting to skip the thinking that

52
00:02:44,519 --> 00:02:46,675
used to be forced on you by the expense.

53
00:02:46,675 --> 00:02:51,093
A three-thousand dollar shoot makes you plan the brief carefully, because you only

54
00:02:51,093 --> 00:02:53,679
get one take, and everyone in the room knows it.

55
00:02:53,679 --> 00:02:58,097
Twenty free-feeling statics tempt you to skip that discipline entirely, because no

56
00:02:58,097 --> 00:03:00,899
single one of them feels like it's risking anything.

57
00:03:00,899 --> 00:03:03,647
But cheap and low-stakes are not the same property.

58
00:03:03,647 --> 00:03:08,172
Any one of those twenty statics can still say something untrue about the compression

59
00:03:08,172 --> 00:03:12,483
spec, still carry a claim Meta or TikTok will reject, or still be the one Dana's

60
00:03:12,483 --> 00:03:15,877
compliance instinct would have caught if she'd looked properly.

61
00:03:15,877 --> 00:03:17,170
Cheap made more of them.

62
00:03:17,170 --> 00:03:20,080
It didn't make any single one of them safe by default.

63
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:22,181
So what actually has to happen instead?

64
00:03:22,181 --> 00:03:26,599
The brief and the pre-flight check get more important, not less, precisely because

65
00:03:26,599 --> 00:03:30,856
nothing expensive is stopping a bad idea from reaching a real customer anymore.

66
00:03:30,856 --> 00:03:32,795
Sections 2 and 6 build exactly that.

67
00:03:33,345 --> 00:03:35,772
So here's the actual shift, stated precisely.

68
00:03:35,772 --> 00:03:40,195
It used to be that having the budget to shoot good creative was the advantage, the

69
00:03:40,195 --> 00:03:43,054
"moat" that kept slower competitors from catching up.

70
00:03:43,054 --> 00:03:47,423
Most of your competitors couldn't afford to try twenty angles, so trying five was

71
00:03:47,423 --> 00:03:48,933
already ahead of the market.

72
00:03:48,933 --> 00:03:51,899
Production budget was that moat, and it was a real one.

73
00:03:51,899 --> 00:03:53,517
That advantage is mostly gone.

74
00:03:53,517 --> 00:03:56,753
Everyone can generate now, at roughly the same cost you can.

75
00:03:56,753 --> 00:03:59,774
So if generation itself isn't the edge anymore, what is?

76
00:03:59,774 --> 00:04:04,250
What's left as an edge is the machine around the generation: the brief that decides

77
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:08,565
what's worth making, the check that keeps a bad one from spending money, and the

78
00:04:08,565 --> 00:04:11,909
test that reads the result honestly instead of guessing at it.

79
00:04:11,909 --> 00:04:16,386
That machine is what the rest of this course builds, piece by piece, using Harlan's

80
00:04:16,386 --> 00:04:18,651
account as the working example throughout.

81
00:04:19,201 --> 00:04:23,836
Next lecture stays with the numbers, because "generate more variants" isn't useful

82
00:04:23,836 --> 00:04:27,849
advice until you know how many is enough, and how many is wasted spend.

83
00:04:27,849 --> 00:04:32,087
That's the volume maths, and it's a smaller number than most people expect.

84
00:04:32,087 --> 00:04:36,100
Before that, do the five-minute version of what Dana already had to do.

85
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:40,735
Open a note and write down your own price, your own cost to deliver it, and what's

86
00:04:40,735 --> 00:04:41,808
actually left over.

87
00:04:41,808 --> 00:04:43,900
That's not homework for its own sake.

88
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:48,308
It's the exact input the brief in Section 2 needs, and having it ready turns a

89
00:04:48,308 --> 00:04:52,603
five-minute Tuesday-night task into a head start on everything that follows.
